{"document":{"type":"root","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Facets repeat one encoding across groups. Multiple-view compositions place\ndifferent but related encodings together. Both reduce the amount of data one\nplot must carry, but they answer different questions."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use facets when every panel has the same role. Use distinct linked views when\none panel provides context, a marginal distribution, controls, or detail for\nanother."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"choose-the-layout","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Choose the layout"}]},{"type":"table","align":[null,null],"header":[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Reader question"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Start with"}]}],"rows":[[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How does the same distribution differ across cohorts?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shared-scale facets"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How does a bivariate relationship relate to each marginal shape?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scatterplot with marginal histograms"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Which portion of a long time domain should the detail view show?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Focus-plus-context views"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Must every group use a different scale or mark composition?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Independent named chart views"}]}],[{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Should selecting one view filter or highlight another?"}]},{"type":"tableCell","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Linked views with shared application state"}]}]]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"link","href":"../guides/faceting-and-composition.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Faceting and Composition"}]},{"type":"text","value":" owns the\nimplementation boundary. This page helps choose a layout and verify its\ncomparison semantics."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"repeat-one-encoding-by-group","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Repeat one encoding by group"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Faceted distributions give each cohort its own panel while preserving a common\nbinning and positional scale. The reader can compare both local shape and\nabsolute position without decoding overlapping fills."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"51-faceted-distributions","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use shared domains when position should be directly comparable. Independent\ndomains can make local variation easier to see, but they can also exaggerate\nsmall differences. If independent scales are intentional, give panels their\nown guides and state the policy."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep the denominator visible by grouping both transforms:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"const bins = normalize(\n  binX(rows, {\n    value: 'body_mass_g',\n    by: 'species',\n    thresholds,\n    outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n  }),\n  {\n    value: 'count',\n    by: 'species',\n    basis: 'sum',\n    as: 'proportion',\n  },\n)","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"inlineCode","value":"binX"},{"type":"text","value":" owns aggregate lineage and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"normalize"},{"type":"text","value":" divides each species by its own\ncount. Omit the second "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"by"},{"type":"text","value":" only when the global population is the intended\ndenominator."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"add-marginal-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Add marginal context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A scatterplot with marginal histograms combines three roles:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The central plot shows the joint relationship."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The top histogram shows the x distribution."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The side histogram shows the y distribution."}]}]}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"57-scatter-marginal-histograms","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"All regions can derive from the same raw observations, but they do not share\nthe same marks or plot rectangle. This example uses public "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"binX"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"binY"},{"type":"text","value":"\ntransforms inside three ordinary chart definitions. Place them with\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"composeViews"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"grid"},{"type":"text","value":", then use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"shareX"},{"type":"text","value":" and "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"shareY"},{"type":"text","value":" to match the\nscatterplot's resolved scales and plot ranges. "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"viewGrid"},{"type":"text","value":" is concise syntax for\nthe same non-overlapping layout."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use separate chart hosts when each view needs independent interaction or an\nindependent accessible label. See\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../reference/view-composition.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"View Composition"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for the single-figure\nlayout contract."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"overlay-a-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Overlay a summary"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"An inset can use a different coordinate system from the chart behind it. This\nplaces a donut summary over a Cartesian detail chart without teaching either\nchild about the other:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { composeViews, fill, inset, layer } from '@tanstack/charts/view'\n\nconst definition = composeViews({\n  views: {\n    detail: detailDefinition,\n    summary: donutDefinition,\n  },\n  layout: layer(\n    fill('detail'),\n    inset('summary', {\n      relativeTo: 'detail',\n      anchor: 'top-right',\n      width: 160,\n      height: 160,\n      offset: 12,\n    }),\n  ),\n})","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The inset is anchored to the complete detail frame. It is clipped to its own\nframe and shrinks proportionally when the host is too small. Because the child\nscenes share one outer interaction model, empty inset space and the donut hole\nleave detail geometry behind them eligible for focus."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"pair-detail-with-context","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pair detail with context"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A focus-plus-context layout uses a compact overview to control the explicit\ndomain of a larger detail chart. The selection is semantic application state,\nnot a rectangle stored inside one renderer."}]},{"type":"component","name":"chart-example","attributes":{"id":"83-focus-context-window","height":"480"},"children":[]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The overview should retain the complete domain. The detail should receive the\nselected start and end as its configured scale domain. Pointer, touch, and\nkeyboard controls should update the same semantic values, and those values\nshould survive data revisions and resizes."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"See "},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/interactions-and-selections.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Interactions and Selections"}]},{"type":"text","value":" for\ncontrolled brushes, zoom, and linked state."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"coordinate-views-by-values-not-pixels","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Coordinate views by values, not pixels"}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Shared selection, cursor, category, or domain state belongs in the application:"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":true,"start":1,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"A view emits a semantic value through focus, selection, or a controlled\ngesture."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Application state validates and stores that value."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each view derives its own definition and configured scales."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Each chart compiles a new scene through its normal update path."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Do not query one SVG for a pixel and apply that pixel directly to another view.\nDifferent margins, widths, orientations, and scales can represent the same\nsemantic value at different coordinates."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For focus-driven cursors, "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"createChartCursor"},{"type":"text","value":" provides that application-owned\nstate without an overlay or callback relay:"}]},{"type":"code","value":"import { createChartCursor, cursorHost } from '@tanstack/charts/cursor'\n\nconst sharedDate = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\nconst cursor = {\n  use: cursorHost,\n  controller: sharedDate,\n  mode: 'focus' as const,\n  match: 'x' as const,\n  pin: true,\n}\n\nconst current = defineChart(currentSpec, { cursor })\nconst previous = defineChart(previousSpec, { cursor })","lang":"ts"},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Pointer, responder, keyboard, or accessibility focus in either browser or\nReact Native chart stores the semantic date. Every subscriber maps that date\nthrough its own x scale, resolves its local focus group, and paints its own\n"},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"crosshair({ y: false })"},{"type":"text","value":". Widths, margins, and y domains can differ. A pinned\ncursor remains shared until another activation, an escape action, or a\nprogrammatic clear dismisses it."}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use "},{"type":"inlineCode","value":"anchor: 'value'"},{"type":"text","value":" when setting a controller programmatically for semantic\nsync. Normalized or scene anchors intentionally share relative or local pixel\ncoordinates instead."}]},{"type":"heading","depth":2,"id":"production-checks","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Production checks"}]},{"type":"list","ordered":false,"items":[{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Use one repeated encoding for true facets; use named views when roles differ."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Place every named composed view exactly once."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Make shared versus independent domains explicit."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Keep bin boundaries, group order, color meaning, and units comparable."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Give each independently interactive view its own accessible label."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Preserve selection and viewport state across data and size updates."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Avoid duplicating a full axis on every panel when shared outer guides are\naccurate."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Verify panel order at narrow widths in\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../guides/responsive-charts.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Responsive Charts"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}]},{"type":"listItem","children":[{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Provide a table or textual summary when exact cross-panel comparison matters."}]}]}]},{"type":"paragraph","children":[{"type":"text","value":"For the underlying row and channel model, see\n"},{"type":"link","href":"../concepts/data-and-channels.md","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Data and Channels"}]},{"type":"text","value":"."}]}],"headings":[{"id":"choose-the-layout","text":"Choose the layout","level":2},{"id":"repeat-one-encoding-by-group","text":"Repeat one encoding by group","level":2},{"id":"add-marginal-context","text":"Add marginal context","level":2},{"id":"overlay-a-summary","text":"Overlay a summary","level":2},{"id":"pair-detail-with-context","text":"Pair detail with context","level":2},{"id":"coordinate-views-by-values-not-pixels","text":"Coordinate views by values, not pixels","level":2},{"id":"production-checks","text":"Production checks","level":2}]},"frameworks":[],"downloadMarkdown":"\nFacets repeat one encoding across groups. Multiple-view compositions place\ndifferent but related encodings together. Both reduce the amount of data one\nplot must carry, but they answer different questions.\n\nUse facets when every panel has the same role. Use distinct linked views when\none panel provides context, a marginal distribution, controls, or detail for\nanother.\n\n## Choose the layout\n\n| Reader question                                                  | Start with                                 |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| How does the same distribution differ across cohorts?            | Shared-scale facets                        |\n| How does a bivariate relationship relate to each marginal shape? | Scatterplot with marginal histograms       |\n| Which portion of a long time domain should the detail view show? | Focus-plus-context views                   |\n| Must every group use a different scale or mark composition?      | Independent named chart views              |\n| Should selecting one view filter or highlight another?           | Linked views with shared application state |\n\n[Faceting and Composition](../guides/faceting-and-composition.md) owns the\nimplementation boundary. This page helps choose a layout and verify its\ncomparison semantics.\n\n## Repeat one encoding by group\n\nFaceted distributions give each cohort its own panel while preserving a common\nbinning and positional scale. The reader can compare both local shape and\nabsolute position without decoding overlapping fills.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=51-faceted-distributions height=480 -->\n\nUse shared domains when position should be directly comparable. Independent\ndomains can make local variation easier to see, but they can also exaggerate\nsmall differences. If independent scales are intentional, give panels their\nown guides and state the policy.\n\nKeep the denominator visible by grouping both transforms:\n\n```ts\nconst bins = normalize(\n  binX(rows, {\n    value: 'body_mass_g',\n    by: 'species',\n    thresholds,\n    outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n  }),\n  {\n    value: 'count',\n    by: 'species',\n    basis: 'sum',\n    as: 'proportion',\n  },\n)\n```\n\n`binX` owns aggregate lineage and `normalize` divides each species by its own\ncount. Omit the second `by` only when the global population is the intended\ndenominator.\n\n## Add marginal context\n\nA scatterplot with marginal histograms combines three roles:\n\n- The central plot shows the joint relationship.\n- The top histogram shows the x distribution.\n- The side histogram shows the y distribution.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=57-scatter-marginal-histograms height=480 -->\n\nAll regions can derive from the same raw observations, but they do not share\nthe same marks or plot rectangle. This example uses public `binX` and `binY`\ntransforms inside three ordinary chart definitions. Place them with\n`composeViews` and `grid`, then use `shareX` and `shareY` to match the\nscatterplot's resolved scales and plot ranges. `viewGrid` is concise syntax for\nthe same non-overlapping layout.\n\nUse separate chart hosts when each view needs independent interaction or an\nindependent accessible label. See\n[View Composition](../reference/view-composition.md) for the single-figure\nlayout contract.\n\n## Overlay a summary\n\nAn inset can use a different coordinate system from the chart behind it. This\nplaces a donut summary over a Cartesian detail chart without teaching either\nchild about the other:\n\n```ts\nimport { composeViews, fill, inset, layer } from '@tanstack/charts/view'\n\nconst definition = composeViews({\n  views: {\n    detail: detailDefinition,\n    summary: donutDefinition,\n  },\n  layout: layer(\n    fill('detail'),\n    inset('summary', {\n      relativeTo: 'detail',\n      anchor: 'top-right',\n      width: 160,\n      height: 160,\n      offset: 12,\n    }),\n  ),\n})\n```\n\nThe inset is anchored to the complete detail frame. It is clipped to its own\nframe and shrinks proportionally when the host is too small. Because the child\nscenes share one outer interaction model, empty inset space and the donut hole\nleave detail geometry behind them eligible for focus.\n\n## Pair detail with context\n\nA focus-plus-context layout uses a compact overview to control the explicit\ndomain of a larger detail chart. The selection is semantic application state,\nnot a rectangle stored inside one renderer.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=83-focus-context-window height=480 -->\n\nThe overview should retain the complete domain. The detail should receive the\nselected start and end as its configured scale domain. Pointer, touch, and\nkeyboard controls should update the same semantic values, and those values\nshould survive data revisions and resizes.\n\nSee [Interactions and Selections](../guides/interactions-and-selections.md) for\ncontrolled brushes, zoom, and linked state.\n\n## Coordinate views by values, not pixels\n\nShared selection, cursor, category, or domain state belongs in the application:\n\n1. A view emits a semantic value through focus, selection, or a controlled\n   gesture.\n2. Application state validates and stores that value.\n3. Each view derives its own definition and configured scales.\n4. Each chart compiles a new scene through its normal update path.\n\nDo not query one SVG for a pixel and apply that pixel directly to another view.\nDifferent margins, widths, orientations, and scales can represent the same\nsemantic value at different coordinates.\n\nFor focus-driven cursors, `createChartCursor` provides that application-owned\nstate without an overlay or callback relay:\n\n```ts\nimport { createChartCursor, cursorHost } from '@tanstack/charts/cursor'\n\nconst sharedDate = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\nconst cursor = {\n  use: cursorHost,\n  controller: sharedDate,\n  mode: 'focus' as const,\n  match: 'x' as const,\n  pin: true,\n}\n\nconst current = defineChart(currentSpec, { cursor })\nconst previous = defineChart(previousSpec, { cursor })\n```\n\nPointer, responder, keyboard, or accessibility focus in either browser or\nReact Native chart stores the semantic date. Every subscriber maps that date\nthrough its own x scale, resolves its local focus group, and paints its own\n`crosshair({ y: false })`. Widths, margins, and y domains can differ. A pinned\ncursor remains shared until another activation, an escape action, or a\nprogrammatic clear dismisses it.\n\nUse `anchor: 'value'` when setting a controller programmatically for semantic\nsync. Normalized or scene anchors intentionally share relative or local pixel\ncoordinates instead.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Use one repeated encoding for true facets; use named views when roles differ.\n- Place every named composed view exactly once.\n- Make shared versus independent domains explicit.\n- Keep bin boundaries, group order, color meaning, and units comparable.\n- Give each independently interactive view its own accessible label.\n- Preserve selection and viewport state across data and size updates.\n- Avoid duplicating a full axis on every panel when shared outer guides are\n  accurate.\n- Verify panel order at narrow widths in\n  [Responsive Charts](../guides/responsive-charts.md).\n- Provide a table or textual summary when exact cross-panel comparison matters.\n\nFor the underlying row and channel model, see\n[Data and Channels](../concepts/data-and-channels.md).\n","originalRawMarkdown":"---\ntitle: Facets and Multiple Views\ndescription: Choose shared-scale facets, marginal views, and focus-plus-context layouts for coordinated comparisons.\n---\n\nFacets repeat one encoding across groups. Multiple-view compositions place\ndifferent but related encodings together. Both reduce the amount of data one\nplot must carry, but they answer different questions.\n\nUse facets when every panel has the same role. Use distinct linked views when\none panel provides context, a marginal distribution, controls, or detail for\nanother.\n\n## Choose the layout\n\n| Reader question                                                  | Start with                                 |\n| ---------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |\n| How does the same distribution differ across cohorts?            | Shared-scale facets                        |\n| How does a bivariate relationship relate to each marginal shape? | Scatterplot with marginal histograms       |\n| Which portion of a long time domain should the detail view show? | Focus-plus-context views                   |\n| Must every group use a different scale or mark composition?      | Independent named chart views              |\n| Should selecting one view filter or highlight another?           | Linked views with shared application state |\n\n[Faceting and Composition](../guides/faceting-and-composition.md) owns the\nimplementation boundary. This page helps choose a layout and verify its\ncomparison semantics.\n\n## Repeat one encoding by group\n\nFaceted distributions give each cohort its own panel while preserving a common\nbinning and positional scale. The reader can compare both local shape and\nabsolute position without decoding overlapping fills.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=51-faceted-distributions height=480 -->\n\nUse shared domains when position should be directly comparable. Independent\ndomains can make local variation easier to see, but they can also exaggerate\nsmall differences. If independent scales are intentional, give panels their\nown guides and state the policy.\n\nKeep the denominator visible by grouping both transforms:\n\n```ts\nconst bins = normalize(\n  binX(rows, {\n    value: 'body_mass_g',\n    by: 'species',\n    thresholds,\n    outputs: { count: { reduce: 'count' } },\n  }),\n  {\n    value: 'count',\n    by: 'species',\n    basis: 'sum',\n    as: 'proportion',\n  },\n)\n```\n\n`binX` owns aggregate lineage and `normalize` divides each species by its own\ncount. Omit the second `by` only when the global population is the intended\ndenominator.\n\n## Add marginal context\n\nA scatterplot with marginal histograms combines three roles:\n\n- The central plot shows the joint relationship.\n- The top histogram shows the x distribution.\n- The side histogram shows the y distribution.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=57-scatter-marginal-histograms height=480 -->\n\nAll regions can derive from the same raw observations, but they do not share\nthe same marks or plot rectangle. This example uses public `binX` and `binY`\ntransforms inside three ordinary chart definitions. Place them with\n`composeViews` and `grid`, then use `shareX` and `shareY` to match the\nscatterplot's resolved scales and plot ranges. `viewGrid` is concise syntax for\nthe same non-overlapping layout.\n\nUse separate chart hosts when each view needs independent interaction or an\nindependent accessible label. See\n[View Composition](../reference/view-composition.md) for the single-figure\nlayout contract.\n\n## Overlay a summary\n\nAn inset can use a different coordinate system from the chart behind it. This\nplaces a donut summary over a Cartesian detail chart without teaching either\nchild about the other:\n\n```ts\nimport { composeViews, fill, inset, layer } from '@tanstack/charts/view'\n\nconst definition = composeViews({\n  views: {\n    detail: detailDefinition,\n    summary: donutDefinition,\n  },\n  layout: layer(\n    fill('detail'),\n    inset('summary', {\n      relativeTo: 'detail',\n      anchor: 'top-right',\n      width: 160,\n      height: 160,\n      offset: 12,\n    }),\n  ),\n})\n```\n\nThe inset is anchored to the complete detail frame. It is clipped to its own\nframe and shrinks proportionally when the host is too small. Because the child\nscenes share one outer interaction model, empty inset space and the donut hole\nleave detail geometry behind them eligible for focus.\n\n## Pair detail with context\n\nA focus-plus-context layout uses a compact overview to control the explicit\ndomain of a larger detail chart. The selection is semantic application state,\nnot a rectangle stored inside one renderer.\n\n<!-- ::chart-example id=83-focus-context-window height=480 -->\n\nThe overview should retain the complete domain. The detail should receive the\nselected start and end as its configured scale domain. Pointer, touch, and\nkeyboard controls should update the same semantic values, and those values\nshould survive data revisions and resizes.\n\nSee [Interactions and Selections](../guides/interactions-and-selections.md) for\ncontrolled brushes, zoom, and linked state.\n\n## Coordinate views by values, not pixels\n\nShared selection, cursor, category, or domain state belongs in the application:\n\n1. A view emits a semantic value through focus, selection, or a controlled\n   gesture.\n2. Application state validates and stores that value.\n3. Each view derives its own definition and configured scales.\n4. Each chart compiles a new scene through its normal update path.\n\nDo not query one SVG for a pixel and apply that pixel directly to another view.\nDifferent margins, widths, orientations, and scales can represent the same\nsemantic value at different coordinates.\n\nFor focus-driven cursors, `createChartCursor` provides that application-owned\nstate without an overlay or callback relay:\n\n```ts\nimport { createChartCursor, cursorHost } from '@tanstack/charts/cursor'\n\nconst sharedDate = createChartCursor<Date, number>()\nconst cursor = {\n  use: cursorHost,\n  controller: sharedDate,\n  mode: 'focus' as const,\n  match: 'x' as const,\n  pin: true,\n}\n\nconst current = defineChart(currentSpec, { cursor })\nconst previous = defineChart(previousSpec, { cursor })\n```\n\nPointer, responder, keyboard, or accessibility focus in either browser or\nReact Native chart stores the semantic date. Every subscriber maps that date\nthrough its own x scale, resolves its local focus group, and paints its own\n`crosshair({ y: false })`. Widths, margins, and y domains can differ. A pinned\ncursor remains shared until another activation, an escape action, or a\nprogrammatic clear dismisses it.\n\nUse `anchor: 'value'` when setting a controller programmatically for semantic\nsync. Normalized or scene anchors intentionally share relative or local pixel\ncoordinates instead.\n\n## Production checks\n\n- Use one repeated encoding for true facets; use named views when roles differ.\n- Place every named composed view exactly once.\n- Make shared versus independent domains explicit.\n- Keep bin boundaries, group order, color meaning, and units comparable.\n- Give each independently interactive view its own accessible label.\n- Preserve selection and viewport state across data and size updates.\n- Avoid duplicating a full axis on every panel when shared outer guides are\n  accurate.\n- Verify panel order at narrow widths in\n  [Responsive Charts](../guides/responsive-charts.md).\n- Provide a table or textual summary when exact cross-panel comparison matters.\n\nFor the underlying row and channel model, see\n[Data and Channels](../concepts/data-and-channels.md).\n"}
